Attack 1 — lock-path completeness (every writer of active-scenario identity
serialized under sm.mu?): grep for `sm.filename =` in
internal/services/retirement/settings.go (non-test) → all four sites
(SwitchScenario:1887, CreateScenario:1931, and the two delete-active-fallback
sites:2005/2058-area) are inside functions that `sm.mu.Lock()` at entry with
`defer sm.mu.Unlock()`. No writer of sm.filename bypasses the lock. The
locked comparison in SaveWithRevisionIfScenario cannot be raced by any
identity mutator. PASS.

Attack 2 — recalc-after-save reads/renders the wrong scenario: read
recalcAndRender/renderRecalc (handlers.go:171-198). renderRecalc operates on
the `settings` object returned BY the mutate closure (the private, just-saved
copy), not on a fresh "active scenario" re-read — so even a scenario switch
immediately after the locked save cannot cause the render to show the wrong
scenario's data. No poisoned-cache angle found specific to this remedy (no
scenario-keyed cache introduced by this change).

Attack 3 — lost update within the SAME scenario (revision/CAS): confirmed
SaveWithRevisionIfScenario does NOT re-check a revision, only scenario
identity — sync's handleWhatIfSyncApply calls LoadContext OUTSIDE the lock,
mutates the local copy, and SaveWithRevisionIfScenario writes it verbatim
inside the lock with no reload-and-merge. Compared against ApplyOverrides
(settings.go:1187-1229), which DOES load-modify-save entirely inside one
held lock (safe against same-scenario concurrent writers) — an asymmetry.
However: (a) this exact contract (`saveAndRecalc`, handlers.go:200-216) is
the PRE-EXISTING pattern used by 7 other handlers (handlers_rates.go, per
manifest) and by saveAndRecalc itself, unchanged by this task; (b)
`saveAndRecalcIfScenario`'s own doc comment explicitly scopes it as
"saveAndRecalc plus the scenario guard," not a request for general
optimistic-concurrency control; (c) Z2's remit, per the attempt-1 FAIL being
remedied, was specifically the scenario-identity TOCTOU (200 + write to an
un-previewed scenario), not general lost-update protection across the whole
saveAndRecalc family. Ruling: in-contract — PASS-with-finding, not a FAIL.
FINDING for backlog: saveAndRecalc-family handlers (sync apply included)
have no revision-based CAS against concurrent same-scenario writers; if that
matters it is a separate, class-wide task (candidate for X7-style
ApplyOverrides-pattern adoption), not a Z2 defect.

Attack 4 — error mapping / errors.As defeat: statusForMutationError →
statusForScenarioOperationError maps *retirement.ScenarioConflictError to
409 (handlers.go). saveAndRecalcIfScenario returns SaveWithRevisionIfScenario's
error unwrapped straight into recalcAndRender's mutate() closure — no
fmt.Errorf("...: %w", err) wrapping in between that could defeat errors.As
(and even if wrapped, errors.As unwraps %w chains, so this would still be
safe). grep confirms SaveWithRevisionIfScenario has exactly one call site.
PASS.

Attack 5 — seam safety: grep syncApplyRaceTestHook across internal/ — set
only in sync_test.go (defer-reset to nil), declared as unexported
package-level `var ... func()` in sync.go with no env/init/build-tag
activation path, invoked only behind `if syncApplyRaceTestHook != nil`.
Cannot be reached from production code or configuration. PASS.

